  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:49:37.075Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nIndividuals required to file an income tax return include every Filipino citizen residing in the Philippines, every Filipino citizen residing outside the Philippines on income from sources within the Philippines, and every alien residing in the Philippines on income derived from sources within the Philippines. Exemptions apply to individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, provided their income tax has been correctly withheld.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe requirements for filing an individual income tax return are governed by Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC). Specifically, Section 51(A) outlines who must file, while Section 51-A provides for substituted filing for certain employees.\n\n### Interpretation\n1. Every Filipino citizen residing in the Philippines.\n2. Every Filipino citizen residing outside the Philippines, on income sourced from within the Philippines.\n3. Every alien residing in the Philippines, on income derived from sources within the Philippines.\n\nSection 51-A allows for substituted filing for employees receiving purely compensation income from a single employer, where the income tax has been withheld correctly, thus exempting them from the requirement to file a return.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practice, this means that most individuals earning income in the Philippines, whether citizens or aliens, are obligated to file an income tax return. However, employees who receive their income solely from one employer and have had their taxes withheld correctly are relieved from the burden of filing, simplifying compliance for a significant portion of the workforce. This provision aims to streamline the tax filing process for individuals with straightforward income situations.",
